-- title:
--   Full-level moduli deformation ring is the Igusa root ring S[X]/(g)
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a prime $\ell$ with $3 \le \ell$, a nonzero natural number $M'$, and a commutative base ring $A₀$.
--
--   **Equivariance hypotheses over $A₀$.** `hℓ` asserts that for every commutative $A₀$-algebra $T$, every Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$, every variable change $C$ and every [`ModularCurve.LevelPData T`](def/ModularCurve_KatzLevelP.html#L43) $D = (x_P, y_P, x_Q, y_Q)$, if $D$ is an $\ell$-level structure on $W$ — both $(x_P,y_P)$ and $(x_Q,y_Q)$ satisfy the affine Weierstrass equation, the division polynomial `preΨ ℓ` of $W$ vanishes at $x_P$ and at $x_Q$, and both independence elements $\prod_{a=1}^{(\ell-1)/2}\bigl(x_Q\,(W.\Psi^2_a)(x_P) - (W.\Phi_a)(x_P)\bigr)$ and its mirror with $x_P, x_Q$ exchanged are units — then the transported datum `D.variableChange C` is an $\ell$-level structure on $C \bullet W$. `hM` asserts, again for all commutative $A₀$-algebras $T$, that `IsGamma0PowAt W p k h` is preserved by variable change, the transported polynomial being `kernelVariableChangeDeg C (gamma0PowDeg p k) h`; here `IsGamma0PowAt W p k h` is `W.IsTwoKernel h` when $p^k = 2$ and otherwise the conjunction of $\deg h \le \varphi(p^k)/2$, $h$ having coefficient $1$ in degree $\varphi(p^k)/2$, the divisibility $h \cdot \mathrm{pre}\Psi_{p^{k-1}} \mid \mathrm{pre}\Psi_{p^k}$, and $h \mid W.\mathrm{smulNumerator}\,a\,(\varphi(p^k)/2)\,h$ for all $a$ with $2 \le a \le (p^k-1)/2$ and $p \nmid a$.
--
--   **Group law and transport over $A₀$.** $\mathcal G$ is a family `GroupLaws A₀`, assigning to each $A₀$-algebra $T$ and each projective Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$ with unit discriminant a relative group law on the graded Proj model `projModelStrCR W`; `h𝒢` says each of these group laws admits a chord–tangent evaluation (an equivalence, for every field extension, between $T$-points of the Proj model and affine points of the base-changed curve, additive and compatible with Galois twists), and `h𝒢O` says each admits an origin chart section with vanishing $x/y$ and $z/y$ coordinates. $\mathcal T$ is a `LevelTransport A₀ 𝒢 q`, a functorial transport of raw Drinfeld pairs (a curve together with two sections $P$, $Q$) along algebra maps and variable changes preserving the Drinfeld-level condition, and `h𝒯` is its section-transport property: the transported sections pull back to the original ones along the Proj maps induced by variable-change homomorphisms, respectively coefficient homomorphisms.
--
--   **The full-level package.** $P₀$ is a `LevelModuliPackageAbs` over $A₀$ for the level moduli datum of `rigidDataPow A₀ ℓ M' q hℓ hM 𝒢 𝒯`, that is, of the rigid Weierstrass datum attached to the product level component $\Gamma_0(M') \times \Gamma(\ell) \times \text{Drinfeld-}\Gamma(q)$: its raw objects over $T$ are a Weierstrass curve with unit discriminant together with a family of kernel polynomials $h_p$ indexed by the prime factors $p$ of $M'$, a `LevelPData T`, and a raw Drinfeld pair, subject to `IsGamma0PowAt` at each $(p, \mathrm{ord}_p M')$, `IsLevelPStructure` at $\ell$, and `RawDrinfeldPair.IsLevel 𝒢 q`; points are raw objects modulo variable change. Thus $P₀$ consists of a ring $P₀.B₀$ over $A₀$ with a universal point `P₀.univ`, every point over any $A₀$-algebra being induced by a unique $A₀$-algebra map out of $P₀.B₀$.
--
--   **The deformation data.** $R$ is a commutative Noetherian local $A₀$-algebra, complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal, with an $A₀$-algebra map $\iota : P₀.B₀ \to R$; $k$ is a field of characteristic $q$ in which $\ell$ and $M'$ are nonzero; $\mathrm{res}_R : R \to k$ is a surjective ring map with kernel the maximal ideal of $R$. Further, $W₀$ is a complete discrete valuation domain with maximal ideal generated by $q$, with a surjection $\mathrm{res}_0 : W₀ \to k$ whose kernel is the maximal ideal, and $R$ is a $W₀$-algebra, $W₀$ an $A₀$-algebra, in a scalar tower over $A₀$, with `hresR₀` saying $\mathrm{res}_R \circ \mathrm{algebraMap}\,W₀\,R = \mathrm{res}_0$. The clause `hfac` is the deformation-theoretic universality of $R$: for every Artinian local $W₀$- and $A₀$-algebra $T$ in the tower, every surjection $\mathrm{res}_T : T \to k$ with kernel the maximal ideal and with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \mathrm{algebraMap}\,W₀\,T = \mathrm{res}_0$, and every $A₀$-algebra map $\varphi : P₀.B₀ \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \varphi = \mathrm{res}_R \circ \iota$, there is a unique $W₀$-algebra map $\Phi : R \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \Phi = \mathrm{res}_R$ and $\Phi \circ \iota = \varphi$.
--
--   **The étale side over $W₀$.** The hypotheses `hℓ'` and `hM'` repeat `hℓ` and `hM` for commutative $W₀$-algebras, and `h𝒢r`, `h𝒢Or` state that the scalar restriction of $\mathcal G$ to $W₀$ is chord–tangent and has the origin as identity. $P^{\mathrm{et}}$ is a `LevelModuliPackageAbs` over $W₀$ for the level moduli datum of the rigid datum of $\Gamma_0(M') \times \Gamma(\ell) \times (\text{trivial})$; $x^{\mathrm{et}}$ is a raw object over $P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀$ (a curve with unit discriminant, kernel polynomials $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{level}.1$, and a level-$\ell$ datum $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{level}.2.1$, together with the level conditions), and `hxet` says its class equals $P^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{univ}$.
--
--   **The $q$-torsion cover.** $CQ$ is a commutative ring which is both a $P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀$-algebra and a $W₀$-algebra in a scalar tower, and $Q^{\mathrm{u}}$ is a $CQ$-point of the Proj model of $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$, i.e. a scheme map over $\mathrm{Spec}$ of $\mathrm{algebraMap}\,P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀\,CQ$. The hypothesis `hQu` says that $q \cdot Q^{\mathrm{u}}$ is the identity section for the restricted group law, and `hrep` says that $CQ$ represents $q$-torsion: for every $P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀$-algebra $T$ and every $T$-point $Q$ of the same Proj model, $q \cdot Q$ is the identity section if and only if there is a unique $P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀$-algebra map $\chi : CQ \to T$ such that $\mathrm{Spec}$ of $\chi$ followed by $Q^{\mathrm{u}}$ equals $Q$.
--
--   **The étale-side complete local ring.** $S$ is a commutative Noetherian local $W₀$-algebra, complete for its maximal-ideal topology, with a $W₀$-algebra map $\iota_S : CQ \to S$, a surjection $\mathrm{res}_S : S \to k$ with kernel the maximal ideal, compatible with $\mathrm{res}_0$ on $W₀$ (`hresS₀`), and `hfacS` the corresponding universality: every $W₀$-algebra map $CQ \to T$ into an Artinian local $W₀$-algebra $T$ with residue data as above and agreeing with $\mathrm{res}_S \circ \iota_S$ after reduction factors through a unique residue-compatible $W₀$-algebra map $S \to T$ extending it.
--
--   **The Igusa polynomial.** $F_S$ is a formal group over $S$ whose power series is the fixed formal group law `formalGroupLawFixed` of $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ base changed to $S$ along $\iota_S$ composed with $\mathrm{algebraMap}\,P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀\,CQ$. The data $g \in S[X]$ and $v \in S\llbracket X\rrbracket$ satisfy: $g$ is monic of degree $q-1$; all coefficients of $g$ in degrees $< q-1$ lie in the maximal ideal of $S$; the constant coefficient of $g$ equals $q\,w$ for some unit $w$ of $S$; $v$ is a unit; and the $q$-th series of $F_S$ (defined by `nthSeries 0 = 0` and `nthSeries (n+1)` the substitution of $(\mathrm{nthSeries}\,n, X)$ into $F_S$, so the multiplication-by-$q$ series) factors as $X \cdot g \cdot v$. Finally `hdom` requires the root ring `AdjoinRoot g` $= S[X]/(g)$ to be a domain and `hqS` that $q \ne 0$ in $S$.
--
--   **The residual point in normal position.** $k$ is an $A₀$-algebra, a $W₀$-algebra in a tower over $A₀$, and a $P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀$-algebra, with `hk₀` identifying $\mathrm{algebraMap}\,W₀\,k$ with $\mathrm{res}_0$. The maps $\rho : P₀.B₀ \to k$ and $\psi^{\mathrm{et}} : P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀ \to k$ are algebra maps over $A₀$, respectively $W₀$, with $\rho = \mathrm{res}_R \circ \iota$ (`hρ`), $\psi^{\mathrm{et}} = \mathrm{res}_S \circ \iota_S \circ \mathrm{algebraMap}\,P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀\,CQ$ (`hψet`), and $\psi^{\mathrm{et}}$ equal to $\mathrm{algebraMap}\,P^{\mathrm{et}}.B₀\,k$ (`hψalg`). The discriminant of the reduced curve $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ base changed along $\psi^{\mathrm{et}}$ is a unit (`hΔk`), and $Q_k$ is a section of its Proj model over $\mathrm{Spec}\,k$. The pinning hypothesis `hQk_pin` asserts the existence of a graded ring homomorphism $\phi$ from the Proj grading of $x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{curve}$ to that of its base change along $\psi^{\mathrm{et}}$, together with a containment of the irrelevant ideal of the target in the image of the irrelevant ideal of the source, such that $\phi$ is a coefficient homomorphism for $\psi^{\mathrm{et}}$ (classes of constants $a$ go to classes of constants $\psi^{\mathrm{et}}(a)$ and the classes of the three coordinates are preserved) and $Q_k$ followed by $\mathrm{Proj.map}\,\phi$ equals $\mathrm{Spec}$ of $\mathrm{res}_S \circ \iota_S$ followed by $Q^{\mathrm{u}}$; `hQk` requires $Q_k$ to differ from the identity section of the group law over $k$. The level conditions over $k$ are: `hhk`, that for each prime $p$ dividing $M'$ the reduced polynomial $(x^{\mathrm{et}}.\mathrm{level}.1\,p)$ satisfies `IsGamma0PowAt` for the reduced curve at $(p, \mathrm{ord}_p M')$; `hDk`, that the reduced level-$\ell$ datum is an $\ell$-level structure on the reduced curve; and `hyk`, that the raw Drinfeld pair consisting of the reduced curve, the identity section and $Q_k$ is of level $q$ for $\mathcal G$, i.e. its basis divisor coincides with the $q$-torsion ideal. The compatibility `hρyk` states that the image of `P₀.univ` under $\rho$ in the points of the full-level datum over $k$ is the class of the raw object made up of the reduced curve with `hΔk`, the reduced $\Gamma_0(M')$ kernels, the reduced level-$\ell$ datum and the Drinfeld pair (reduced curve, identity section, $Q_k$), with the level proofs $\langle$`hhk`, `hDk`, `hyk`$\rangle$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under these hypotheses the type of $W₀$-algebra isomorphisms $R \simeq S[X]/(g)$ is nonempty; that is, $R$ and the root ring `AdjoinRoot g` (a $W₀$-algebra through $S$) are isomorphic as $W₀$-algebras.
--
--   This identifies the universal ring $R$ of the full-level moduli problem $\Gamma_0(M') \times \Gamma(\ell) \times \text{Drinfeld-}\Gamma(q)$, taken at a residual point in the stated normal position over a residue field of characteristic $q$, with the Igusa-type root ring $S[X]/(g)$ obtained from the étale-side ring $S$ and the degree $q-1$ Eisenstein factor $g$ of the multiplication-by-$q$ series of the formal group. It is used in the form where $S$ is a power series ring over $W₀$, yielding an explicit presentation of $R$.
